<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Sancocho!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe objects using basic words or phrases, provided they can prepare in advance. Interactive: Interact with questions and answers about a classroom topic.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe food items using basic phrases (e.g., "It's a red apple."). Can ask and answer questions in simple discussions about food with peers (e.g., "What's your favorite food?" "I like ice cream."). Can describe basic food items by following a model provided in advance (e.g., "I like bananas. They are sweet.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of various food items (e.g., sancocho, rice with chicken, apple, banana, juice, water, sandwich, soup). The teacher holds up each flashcard and asks: "What's this?" and "Do you like [food item]?" Students respond chorally or individually with "Yes, I do!" or "No, I don't." The teacher also leads a quick TPR activity: "Eat an apple!" "Drink some water!" "Taste the soup!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing likes and dislikes using simple sentences: "I like sancocho. It is tasty." "I like apples. They are sweet." "I don't like soup, but I like rice with chicken." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack," encouraging clear articulation and blending of phonemes like /ˈæ-p.əl/ in "apple" and /ˈw-ɔːt-ər/ in "water" as they speak the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with visual aids and questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing an apple flashcard) "Is this a banana?" (No!) "Is it sweet?" (Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing sancocho) "Do I like sancocho?" (Yes!) "Is sancocho a drink?" (No!)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two children, Ana and Ben, discussing their favorite foods for lunch and dinner. The dialogue uses previously learned vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup) and introduces the target grammar (WH- and yes/no questions, "I like...", "I like...but I don't...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Dialogue Example:</i><br>
                Ana: "Hi, Ben! What's your favorite food for lunch?"<br>
                Ben: "Hi, Ana! I like rice with chicken. It's tasty!"<br>
                Ana: "Do you like sancocho?"<br>
                Ben: "Yes, I do! Sancocho is healthy. But I don't like soup for dinner."<br>
                Ana: "I like bananas, but I don't like apples very much."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information about preferences: Expressing likes and dislikes about food (e.g., "I like apples."), and asking about others' food preferences (e.g., "Do you like oranges?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Oral):</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, then asks simple oral questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Ben's favorite food for lunch?" (Rice with chicken.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Ana like apples?" (No, not very much.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is sancocho healthy?" (Yes!)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice forming WH- and yes/no questions, responding with present simple statements (I like/don't like), and using compound sentences with 'but'. They use vocabulary: food nouns (apple, banana, sandwich, sancocho, rice with chicken, soup, snack), verbs (eat, drink, taste), and adjectives (tasty, healthy, sweet, favorite, hungry). Pronunciation focus on /f/ and /s/ sounds and blending.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher shows flashcards of food items and provides sentence frames orally or on the board. Students complete the sentences orally, focusing on correct grammar and pronunciation.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like ____. It is ____." (e.g., "I like apples. They are sweet.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like ____, but I don't like ____." (e.g., "I like sancocho, but I don't like juice.")</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher listens for the /f/ sound in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack" as students speak.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Ask Your Partner (Guided Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs with a worksheet containing images of 4-5 food items. One student asks, "Do you like [food item]?" and the partner responds, "Yes, I do. I like it. It's tasty!" or "No, I don't. I don't like it." They take turns. Teacher monitors and corrects pronunciation, especially blending sounds in words like "apple" and "water."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Favorite Food Chain:</b> Students stand in a circle. The first student says, "My favorite food is sancocho." The next student says, "His/Her favorite food is sancocho, and my favorite food is rice with chicken." The chain continues, adding new food items and practicing "I like...but I don't..." as students become more comfortable.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Food Preference Interview" Role-play.</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of food flashcards (e.g., apple, banana, sandwich, sancocho, soup, juice, water, rice with chicken) and a simple conversation guide with prompts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme ("I Like Sancocho!") and Scenario ("It's Time to Eat."):</b> Students take turns interviewing each other about their food preferences for different meals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What's your favorite food for lunch?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "My favorite food for lunch is [rice with chicken]. I like it because it's tasty. What about you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I like [sandwiches]. But I don't like [soup]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Do you like [sancocho]?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Yes, I do! It's my favorite. It's healthy!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They switch roles and repeat the interview. The teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging fluency. Students are encouraged to use adjectives like 'tasty', 'healthy', 'sweet'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' participation and use of target language during the role-play. Students also quickly assess their partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Food Preference Interview" with their partner to another pair or briefly to the whole class (1-2 pairs, time permitting).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear and intelligible pronunciation (especially /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack," and blending of phonemes in words like "apple," "water").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Uses target food vocabulary (e.g., apple, banana, sancocho, sandwich, juice, soup) and adjectives (tasty, healthy, sweet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Correctly uses "I like / I don't like," "Do you like...?", "What's your favorite...?", and "I like...but I don't...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Asks and answers questions clearly and politely with a partner.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple ticket before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask "Do you like...?" (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say what food I like. (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">My favorite food is ________________.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new food words did you use today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was fun about talking about food with your friends?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students engaged well in the speaking activities, especially the chain drill and pair interviews. They successfully used "I like..." and "Do you like...?" Some students needed more prompting for "I like...but I don't..." The pronunciation of /f/ and /s/ needs continued reinforcement. For next lesson, I will ensure visual aids are readily available to support writing about food, perhaps with a word bank to aid sentence construction based on preferences discussed today.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job speaking about your favorite foods today! In our next lesson, we will *write* about the foods we like and what we eat for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, using all the words we practiced today."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>